<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arachosia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arachosia was an ancient region in present-day southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, centred around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>. It was an Achaemenid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> and was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Around 303 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> ceded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, as part of the settlement that shaped the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arachosia.jpg" alt="Arachosia" width="320" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThreeDottedDomino · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Arachosia_around_500_BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Arachosia, in Old Persian Harauvatiš, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, centred mainly on the Arghandab River, a tributary of the Helmand, and reaching east as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. The Greek name comes from Arachōtós, the Greek name of that river, and the Old Persian form is the etymological equivalent of Sarasvatī in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-sanskrit">Vedic Sanskrit</a>. The region bordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drangiana">Drangiana</a> to the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paropamisadae">Paropamisadae</a> to the north, Hindush to the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedrosia">Gedrosia</a> to the south.</p><p>Ancient writers list several cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-charax">Isidore of Charax</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and Pliny call <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on the river Arachotus the metropolis of Arachosia; it is often wrongly identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, but a clay tablet inscription has shown that Kandahar traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> well before Alexander. Ptolemy names an earlier capital, Arachotus, said to have been originally called Cophen and identified today with Arghandab, north-west of Kandahar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region first appears in the Elamite Persepolis fortification tablets and later in inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>, and it supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> for Darius's palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. In the Behistun inscription Darius recounts that its governor Vivana defeated a rebel Persian three times and kept the province in his hands. Around 330 BCE Alexander the Great commissioned Alexandria Arachosia as its new capital, on the site of an earlier Persian fortress, and appointed his generals as governors. In 316 BCE Antigonus I sent most of the veteran Argyraspides to the satrap Sibyrtius to guard the frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with orders to send them out in small groups on dangerous missions so that their numbers dwindled.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-diadochi">Wars of the Diadochi</a> the region passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, which ceded it to the Mauryan Empire in return for an alliance. Later it fell in turn to the Greco-Bactrian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> kingdoms, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a>, the Arsacids and Indo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, and the Kushans, who held it until about 230 CE, when the second Persian Empire defeated them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Arachosia then came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of that empire, the Kushanshas, who were driven out in 420 by the Chionites, founders of the Kidarite Kingdom. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> replaced the Kidarites in the 460s and were defeated in 565 by a coalition of Persian and Turkish armies. Around 870 the Kushano-Hephthalites were replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a>, then by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a> and the Ghaznavids in the early 11th century. Arab geographers called the region Rokhaj.</p><p>Its inhabitants were Iranian peoples, called Arachosians or Arachoti and also Pactyans, a name that may refer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a>, though Frye calls the identification a guess that remains unproven. Isidore described its metropolis as Greek even in the 1st century CE, and said the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> called the region White India.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arachosia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His coins have been found in <b>Arachosia</b> and the Kabul valley; he conquered those regions and perhaps Gandhara, though he struck no Indian coins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In a war and treaty with Seleucus I Nicator around 305–303 BCE, he gained <b>Arachosia</b>, Gedrosia and the Paropamisadae, covering much of modern Pakistan and parts of Afghanistan, in return for 500 war elephants, and received the Greek envoy Megasthenes at his court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The resulting Treaty of the Indus ceded the eastern satrapies of Paropamisadae, <b>Arachosia</b> and Gedrosia, in whole or in part, to the Mauryas in exchange for 500 war elephants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Gondophares, a ruler of Iranian, probably Parthian, origin from Sistan, built a kingdom in the early 1st century by taking <b>Arachosia</b> and then Gandhara and Taxila from the Indo-Scythians.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-parthian-kingdom">Indo-Parthian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>They passed through Sistan and <b>Arachosia</b> into the Indus valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a></li></ul></section>
